## Data and definitions

The sample contains **{{ fixtures }} fixtures across {{ competitions }} competition-season groups**, with scheduled kickoffs from **{{ first }} to {{ last }}**. Each fixture has a Polymarket and a Kalshi regular-time 1X2 market linked to the same API-Football fixture. The previous trade-count post used an earlier 1,942-fixture snapshot.

The time reference is **scheduled kickoff**, including for the in-play windows: elapsed wall-clock minutes, halftime included, not the match clock. "In play" means 0–105 minutes after scheduled kickoff; the 55-minute split used in one diagnostic is a clock split, not an observed halftime.

| Term | Meaning here |
| --- | --- |
| Print (trade reference) | Fills within one second on one venue, outcome and taker side, combined into a volume-weighted average price. The pooled leg merges both taker sides. |
| Buy / sell | Always the **taker's** action: a trade record carries only the side that crossed the book, so `buy` is a taker buying YES (printed at the ask) and `sell` a taker selling YES (printed at the bid). Nothing in this part assigns a role: routes, which read the same print from the resting maker's side, are introduced in part 2. |
| Fresh pair | Both venues' latest prints are at most **F seconds** old; a pair's age is the age of its older print. F looks backward. Raising F never changes the prices an observation uses: at a given print, the other leg is always that venue's latest print. F only decides whether the observation is in scope. |
| Observation | A print on either leg while the other leg is fresh. Per-print statistics weight each observation equally; per-second statistics weight it by how long the pair then stays fresh. |
| Forward horizon H | Whether the other venue prints strictly later and within **H seconds** of a trade. H looks forward; F and H are independent. |
| Signed gap | Polymarket price minus Kalshi price, in cents per $1 contract. Negative means the Polymarket reference is lower. |
| Team win | Home and away priced separately, then pooled for reporting. It is not a combined contract. |
| Matchup class | A fixture is **balanced** or **unequal** from its pregame prices: the VWAP of the last 30 pregame minutes on each venue, averaged across venues and normalised to a 1X2 probability. Unequal when \|q~home~ − q~away~\| / (q~home~ + q~away~) exceeds 0.30. In an unequal fixture the favoured side is **strong** and the other **weak**; the draw is always the draw. |

## 1. Where the trading is

{{ chart('3:0') }}

Trades per fixture is the number to keep in mind for everything that follows. The World Cup traded about {{ k.wc_vs_pl }}× the Premier League and {{ k.wc_vs_l1 }}× Ligue 1 per fixture, and Kalshi printed {{ k.k_over_pm_pl }}× as many trades as Polymarket per Premier League fixture and {{ k.k_over_pm_wc }}× per World Cup fixture. A single freshness cutoff therefore keeps a very different share of each group's clock, which is why section 2 selects groups before comparing prices.

### 1X2 within each venue's soccer tape

A 1X2 market prices the regular-time result of one match as three contracts: home win, draw, away win. On Kalshi it is the `game` series of each competition; on Polymarket it is the `moneyline` sports market type, which carries the draw as its third outcome. The table measures this type against everything else in my raw soccer pulls: every market type on both venues, every series and condition, to about August 2026 ({{ k.share.k_gb }} GB and {{ k.share.k_rows_m }} million trade rows on Kalshi, {{ k.share.pm_gb }} GB and {{ k.share.pm_rows_m }} million on Polymarket). Trade rows are raw fills, not the per-second prints used below; contracts are Kalshi `count` and Polymarket `size`. This is the universe the study is cut from, not the linked fixture population above.

{{ share }}

On Kalshi, 1X2 is half the tape by trade rows; the next type, match totals, has {{ k.share.k_total_trades }}. On Polymarket, `moneyline` holds {{ k.share.pm_trades }} of rows, and a further {{ k.share.pm_unknown_trades }} of rows ({{ k.share.pm_unknown_contracts }} of contracts) sit in older markets that carry no market-type tag, some of which are 1X2 markets too, so the Polymarket share is a floor. Totals are next at {{ k.share.pm_totals_trades }}.

## 2. Is there a fresh pair to compare?

Availability is measured over the entire clock of every fixture, outcome and window, including the seconds in which nothing traded. A venue is fresh when its latest print is at most F seconds old.

### 2.1 Share of the clock with both venues fresh

{{ chart('5:0') }}

The World Cup reaches a high dual-fresh share within a few seconds. Club competitions need tens of seconds, and their two in-play halves differ by at most a few percentage points, so they are pooled below. The Women's Champions League 2025/26 group (four fixtures, no Kalshi trades) never has a fresh pair and is omitted from the availability charts.

### 2.2 Where the rest of the clock goes

At any instant, a market is in one of four states: both venues fresh, only Kalshi fresh, only Polymarket fresh, or neither. "Neither fresh" means neither venue traded in the preceding F seconds; it does not mean data are missing.

{{ chart('7:0') }}

At F = 60 s, the Premier League in-play clock splits into {{ k.pl60.both }} both fresh, {{ k.pl60.k }} only Kalshi fresh, {{ k.pl60.pm }} only Polymarket fresh and {{ k.pl60.neither }} neither; the Champions League into {{ k.ucl60.both }}, {{ k.ucl60.k }}, {{ k.ucl60.pm }} and {{ k.ucl60.neither }}. At F = 5 s, both venues are fresh for {{ k.wc5.both }} of the World Cup in-play clock but only {{ k.pl5.both }} of the Premier League's. The excluded clock is mostly one-sided: Kalshi has printed recently and Polymarket has not.

### 2.3 Groups entering the price study

A competition-season × window qualifies when both venues are fresh for at least 70% of its clock at F = 60 s (coverage rounded to a whole percent, which is how the Champions League qualifies at {{ k.ucl60.both }}). Each qualifying group then uses the smallest cutoff among 5, 10 and 60 s that reaches 70%. Four groups qualify:

{{ candidates }}

This is a scope rule for the fresh-pair comparisons, not a ranking of profitability. Groups that do not qualify simply have too little time with both venues recently active for a same-time price comparison; an asynchronous strategy in those groups is a different question.

**Fixture concentration.** Every rate below pools observations across a group's fixtures, so a few busy matches could dominate it. For each group, the curve counts fresh prints on the two cross-side action pairs of section 4 (a Polymarket buy against a Kalshi sell, or the reverse) per fixture at the group's F, busiest fixture first.

{{ chart('9:0') }}

{{ concentration }}

The busiest fixture holds at most {{ k.conc.top1_max }} of its group's observations and the busiest five between {{ k.conc.top5_min }} and {{ k.conc.top5_max }}. The Champions League is the most uneven (Gini {{ k.conc.ucl_gini }}; its busiest tenth of fixtures holds {{ k.conc.ucl_top10pct }}, and its busiest fixture has {{ k.conc.ucl_max_over_median }}× the median fixture's observations), the World Cup in play the most even ({{ k.conc.wc_gini }}). The pooled rates that follow describe their group rather than a handful of matches; their uncertainty is nonetheless closer to the fixture count than to the observation count.

### 2.4 Which leg goes stale

The four states above pool both taker sides of each venue. Section 4 compares prints from one side of each venue, a Polymarket buy against a Kalshi sell for instance, and such a pair is fresh only when both those sides printed within F seconds.

{{ chart('11:0') }}

Per taker side, the picture is one-sided in a different way. In the Premier League at F = 60 s, the four pure pairs are fresh on only {{ k.pl_pair['sell/sell'].both }}–{{ k.pl_pair['buy/buy'].both }} of the in-play clock, against {{ k.pl60.both }} pooled, and the missing leg is each venue's sell side: `PM buy / K sell` spends {{ k.pl_pair['buy/sell'].pm }} of the clock with only Polymarket fresh, because Kalshi's sell-side print is stale, while `PM sell / K buy` spends {{ k.pl_pair['sell/buy'].k }} with only Kalshi fresh. `buy/buy` is the best-covered pair ({{ k.pl_pair['buy/buy'].both }}) and `sell/sell` the worst ({{ k.pl_pair['sell/sell'].both }}). In the World Cup's last pregame hour at F = 10 s the asymmetry is extreme: `PM sell / K buy` is {{ k.wcpre_sellbuy.k }} "only Kalshi fresh". Kalshi's buy-heavy flow, which section 4 sees from the price side, is the same fact seen from the activity side.

### 2.5 After a trade, how soon does the other venue trade?

This asks about trade seconds rather than the whole clock. For each source-venue trade second, does the destination venue print strictly later and within H seconds? Same-second prints do not count as a response, and a trigger is eligible only when its entire H-second future lies inside the window.

{{ chart('13:0') }}

The curves are hockey sticks. After a Kalshi trade second in the Premier League in play, Polymarket prints again within 1 / 5 / 10 / 60 s in {{ k.wait.pl_k[1] }} / {{ k.wait.pl_k[5] }} / {{ k.wait.pl_k[10] }} / {{ k.wait.pl_k[60] }} of eligible cases; the Champions League behaves the same ({{ k.wait.ucl_k[5] }} within 5 s). The reverse direction is faster everywhere ({{ k.wait.pl_pm[5] }} within 5 s in the Premier League), and the World Cup in play is at {{ k.wait.wc_k[5] }} (Kalshi → Polymarket) and {{ k.wait.wc_pm[5] }} (Polymarket → Kalshi) within 5 s. These are waiting probabilities, not evidence that one venue's trade causes the other's.

## 3. How far apart are the last trades?

An observation is a print on either venue while the other venue's latest print is at most F seconds old, at each group's own cutoff. The signed gap is Polymarket minus Kalshi, pooled over both taker sides; a gap of −2 c means the Polymarket reference is 2 c below Kalshi's.

### 3.1 By outcome

{{ chart('16:0') }}

Per print weights each observation equally; per second weights it by how long the pair then stays fresh before the next print or expiry. The centre bucket holds gaps strictly between −1 and +1 c, the adjacent buckets hold the exact one-cent gaps both venues quote, and the end buckets are open at ±4 c. The dashed line marks the mean signed gap per print, placed on the bucket scale. Every panel in this section shares one y axis.

For all outcomes pooled, the Premier League in-play sample has a **{{ k.gap.pl.abs }} c mean absolute gap** with {{ k.gap.pl.ge2 }} of fresh prints at least 2 c apart; the Champions League {{ k.gap.ucl.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.ucl.ge2 }}; the World Cup in play {{ k.gap.wc.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.wc.ge2 }}; the World Cup's last hour {{ k.gap.wcpre.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.wcpre.ge2 }}. These use different cutoffs (60, 60, 5 and 10 s), so they mix activity and freshness effects; section 3.3 separates them.

The gap is not symmetric. In the Premier League, Polymarket prints at least 1 c **below** Kalshi on {{ k.gap.pl.lower }} of observations and at least 1 c **above** on {{ k.gap.pl.higher }}, a pooled mean of {{ k.gap.pl.mean }} c. The draw is more two-sided than team wins: Polymarket is at least 1 c above on {{ k.gap.pl_draw.higher }} of draw prints against {{ k.gap.pl_team.higher }} of team-win prints, and at least 1 c below on {{ k.gap.pl_draw.lower }} against {{ k.gap.pl_team.lower }}.

### 3.2 By matchup class and team role

The same distribution, with each in-play fixture first classed as balanced or unequal from its pregame prices (definitions above). In a balanced fixture the rows are the two team wins and the draw; in an unequal fixture, the strong side, the weak side and the draw. The label is built from the last 30 pregame minutes, so it is fixed before the first in-play observation; it is not applied to the World Cup's last pregame hour, whose prints would be classifying themselves. Unequal fixtures supply {{ k.matchup.unequal_min }}–{{ k.matchup.unequal_max }} of each group's prints; fixtures without a usable label are at most {{ k.matchup.unknown_max }} and are dropped here.

{{ chart('16:1') }}

The favourite of an unequal fixture is where Polymarket prints lowest relative to Kalshi: a mean gap of {{ k.gap.pl_strong.mean }} c in the Premier League and {{ k.gap.ucl_strong.mean }} c in the Champions League, against {{ k.gap.pl_weak.mean }} c and {{ k.gap.ucl_weak.mean }} c on the weak side and {{ k.gap.pl_bal_team.mean }} c and {{ k.gap.ucl_bal_team.mean }} c for team wins in balanced fixtures. The draw is close to zero in every class ({{ k.gap.pl_uneq_draw.mean }} c and {{ k.gap.ucl_uneq_draw.mean }} c in unequal fixtures, {{ k.gap.pl_bal_draw.mean }} c and {{ k.gap.ucl_bal_draw.mean }} c in balanced ones) and two-sided. In the Premier League and the World Cup the weak side has the smallest absolute gap ({{ k.gap.pl_weak.abs }} c and {{ k.gap.wc_weak.abs }} c): it is the lowest-priced of the three contracts, where the two venues' quotes sit closest in cents.

### 3.3 Across the freshness cutoff

The pooled distribution's summary at F = 5, 10 and 60 s for each group. A tighter cutoff keeps fewer prints, but not different ones.

{{ chart('16:2') }}

The mean absolute gap is {{ k.robust.pl_5.abs }} / {{ k.robust.pl_10.abs }} / {{ k.robust.pl_60.abs }} c at F = 5 / 10 / 60 s in the Premier League, {{ k.robust.ucl_5.abs }} / {{ k.robust.ucl_10.abs }} / {{ k.robust.ucl_60.abs }} c in the Champions League and {{ k.robust.wc_5.abs }} / {{ k.robust.wc_10.abs }} / {{ k.robust.wc_60.abs }} c in the World Cup in play; the share of prints at least 2 c apart moves by about a point ({{ k.robust.pl_5.ge2 }} to {{ k.robust.pl_60.ge2 }} in the Premier League). Whatever the cutoff admits or excludes, the two tapes disagree by the same amount. The cutoff is therefore better read as a bound on how long one venue's reference can stand while the other is silent, the exposure of a leg done on one venue before the other prints again, than as something that changes the gap. Part 2 finds the same flatness after fees.

## 4. Which side each trade came from

Two pooled last trades do not describe prices anyone could trade at. A taker buy prints at its venue's ask and a taker sell at its bid, so the nine squares of each panel split the prints by the taker side on each venue; `all` pools both sides and repeats section 3. Each square shows the mean signed gap, the share of prints at least 2 c below and above zero, and the print count; blue means Polymarket printed lower, red higher.

| Pair (PM action / Kalshi action) | Reads |
| --- | --- |
| buy / sell | Polymarket ask-side print against Kalshi bid-side print. Positive is the ordinary state, an ask above the other venue's bid; negative means the two references were crossed. |
| sell / buy | Polymarket bid-side print against Kalshi ask-side print. Negative is the ordinary state; positive means crossed. |
| buy / buy | Both ask-side: how the two venues' asks compare. |
| sell / sell | Both bid-side. |

{{ chart('18:0') }}

The cross-side pairs show the ordinary cross-venue spread, and it is not symmetric. For Premier League team wins, `sell/buy` averages {{ k.pair.pl_team_sellbuy }} c and `buy/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_team_buysell }} c: Polymarket's bid side sits further below Kalshi's ask side than Polymarket's ask side sits above Kalshi's bid side. Pooled over both sides, Polymarket prints lower than Kalshi ({{ k.pair.pl_all_allall }} c in the Premier League, {{ k.pair.wc_all_allall }} c in the World Cup in play): Kalshi's flow is buy-heavy and prints at the ask, the same fact section 2.4 saw as Kalshi's stale sell side. For draws the wider side is the other one (`sell/buy` {{ k.pair.pl_draw_sellbuy }} c, `buy/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_draw_buysell }} c in the Premier League; {{ k.pair.ucl_draw_sellbuy }} c and {{ k.pair.ucl_draw_buysell }} c in the Champions League). In a cross-side square, the tail on the crossed side (at least 2 c below zero for `buy/sell`, at least 2 c above for `sell/buy`) is how often the two references were crossed by at least 2 c, before any fee.

The same-side pairs compare like with like and are small: `buy/buy` {{ k.pair.pl_team_buybuy }} c and `sell/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_team_sellsell }} c for Premier League team wins, `sell/sell` {{ k.pair.pl_draw_sellsell }} c for draws.

Home and away are not separated because their pooled gaps agree to within 0.2 c in every group; the cross-venue mechanics are symmetric between them.

### By matchup class and team role

{{ chart('18:1') }}

Kalshi's premium over Polymarket is concentrated on the favourite. `sell/buy` averages {{ k.pair.ucl_strong_sellbuy }} c on the strong side of unequal Champions League fixtures against {{ k.pair.ucl_weak_sellbuy }} c on the weak side ({{ k.pair.pl_strong_sellbuy }} c and {{ k.pair.pl_weak_sellbuy }} c in the Premier League), while `buy/sell` is narrower on the strong side ({{ k.pair.pl_strong_buysell }} c against {{ k.pair.pl_weak_buysell }} c in the Premier League). On draws, Polymarket's ask side sits highest above Kalshi's bid side in unequal fixtures (`buy/sell` {{ k.pair.ucl_uneq_draw_buysell }} c against {{ k.pair.ucl_bal_draw_buysell }} c in balanced Champions League fixtures).

